          text: '<span class="para-ref">[1.1]</span> In October 1914, the First World War expanded dramatically beyond Europe when the <strong>Ottoman Empire</strong> formally entered the conflict on the side of the Central Powers (Imperial Germany and Austria-Hungary). For over four centuries, the Ottoman sultans ruling from Istanbul had governed a vast empire spanning the Middle East, including modern Turkey, Syria, Lebanon, Palestine, Jordan, and Iraq. The Ottoman decision to enter the war posed a catastrophic strategic threat to the British Empire: Ottoman territory bordered the vital <strong>Suez Canal</strong> in Egypt—the maritime jugular vein connecting Great Britain to its richest colony, India. When Ottoman Sultan Mehmed V declared a religious <em>Jihad</em> (holy war) against the Entente powers, British military planners in London and Cairo feared that millions of Muslim subjects across India and Egypt would rise in mutiny against the British Crown.<br><br><span class="para-ref">[1.2]</span> Desperate to neutralize the Ottoman threat, the British High Commissioner in Cairo, <strong>Sir Henry McMahon</strong>, initiated secret diplomatic negotiations in July 1915 with <strong>Sharif Hussein bin Ali</strong>, the Emir of Mecca and the official guardian of Islam’s most sacred holy sites. Over ten historic letters known as the <strong>McMahon-Hussein Correspondence</strong> (July 1915 – March 1916), McMahon negotiated an anti-Ottoman military alliance. Hussein agreed to lead an armed Arab rebellion against Turkish rule. In return, McMahon explicitly pledged in a crucial letter dated 24 October 1915 that Great Britain would "recognize and support the independence of the Arabs in all the regions within the limits demanded by the Sherif of Mecca"—a territory that Arab leaders understood to encompass the entire Arabian Peninsula, Syria, Mesopotamia, and Palestine, with only vague coastal exclusions.<br><br><span class="para-ref">[1.3]</span> On 10 June 1916, Sharif Hussein fired a ceremonial rifle shot from his palace balcony in Mecca, officially launching the <strong>Great Arab Revolt</strong>. Commanded in the field by Hussein’s sons—Emir Feisal and Emir Abdullah—and aided by British liaison officers including the legendary <strong>T.E. Lawrence ("Lawrence of Arabia")</strong>, Arab tribal warriors waged a devastating guerrilla campaign. Moving on fast camels across the harsh desert, Arab forces ambushed Turkish garrisons and repeatedly dynamited the <strong>Hejaz Railway</strong>—the 800-mile telegraph and train link connecting Damascus to Medina. This brilliant desert campaign pinned down over 30,000 Ottoman troops who were desperately needed to defend Palestine, allowing British imperial forces to prepare a major counter-offensive from Egypt.',
