          text: '<span class="para-ref">[2.1]</span> While Sir Henry McMahon was actively promising national independence to Sharif Hussein in Cairo, British and French diplomats were secretly meeting in London and Petrograd to plan the exact opposite: the imperial dismemberment and colonization of the Middle East. Negotiated between November 1915 and May 1916 by British Middle East specialist <strong>Sir Mark Sykes</strong> and French diplomat <strong>François Georges-Picot</strong>, the clandestine accord became known as the <strong>Sykes-Picot Agreement</strong> (formally the Asia Minor Agreement). The treaty reflected classic nineteenth-century European imperialism: neither diplomat possessed intimate knowledge of the region’s diverse populations, and not a single Arab, Kurdish, or Jewish representative was consulted.<br><br><span class="para-ref">[2.2]</span> Using rulers and coloured pencils, Sykes and Picot drew an arbitrary diagonal line across the map from the Mediterranean port of Acre to Kirkuk in northern Iraq ("a line in the sand") (Source A). Under the agreement, the lands north of the line were allocated to France: the <strong>"Blue Zone"</strong> (direct French colonial administration in coastal Syria, Lebanon, and Cilicia) and <strong>"Area A"</strong> (indirect French control in inland Syria). Lands south of the line fell to Great Britain: the <strong>"Red Zone"</strong> (direct British rule over Mesopotamia, including Baghdad and the Persian Gulf port of Basra, securing critical naval oil supplies) and <strong>"Area B"</strong> (indirect British influence spanning Transjordan to the Persian border). Crucially, the holy lands of <strong>Palestine</strong>—due to conflicting religious and imperial claims—were designated as a "Brown Zone" to be placed under an international administration.<br><br><span class="para-ref">[2.3]</span> The Sykes-Picot Agreement was a breathtaking act of imperial double-dealing. It flagrantly contradicted the promises of Arab sovereignty given to Sharif Hussein only months earlier. The secret pact remained hidden until November 1917, when Vladimir Lenin’s <strong>Bolshevik government</strong> seized power in Russia. Discovering the secret Tsarist and Allied treaties in the imperial archives, Leon Trotsky published the full text of the Sykes-Picot Agreement in the Soviet newspapers <em>Izvestia</em> and <em>Pravda</em>, from which it was reprinted across the global press. The revelation produced profound shock and outrage across the Arab world. Sharif Hussein and Emir Feisal realized with bitter clarity that while Arab soldiers were shedding their blood in the desert fighting Ottoman troops, their British allies had already carved up their homeland as colonial spoils of war.',
