          text: '<span class="para-ref">[4.1]</span> When the Great War concluded in November 1918, the victorious Allied powers faced the colossal task of reorganizing the former Ottoman territories. At the 1919 Paris Peace Conference, American President Woodrow Wilson championed <strong>"national self-determination"</strong>—the principle that all ethnic nationalities had the right to govern themselves. Arab leaders, led by Emir Feisal, traveled to Paris to demand the independent Arab kingdom promised in the McMahon letters. However, British Prime Minister David Lloyd George and French Premier Georges Clemenceau dismissed Arab sovereignty as impractical. Instead, they invented the <strong>Mandate System</strong> under <strong>Article 22 of the League of Nations Covenant</strong>. The Covenant patronizingly categorized former Ottoman peoples as "not yet able to stand by themselves under the strenuous conditions of the modern world", decreeing that their tutelage should be entrusted to "advanced nations" until they were capable of independence.<br><br><span class="para-ref">[4.2]</span> The reality was imperial annexation under international disguise. At the <strong>San Remo Conference (April 1920)</strong>, Britain and France formally carved up the Arab provinces into Class A Mandates (Source D). France was granted mandates over <strong>Syria and Lebanon</strong>, while Great Britain received mandates over <strong>Mesopotamia (Iraq)</strong> and <strong>Palestine (including Transjordan)</strong>. When a Syrian National Congress defiantly proclaimed Emir Feisal as King of an independent constitutional kingdom in Damascus, the French military responded with brute force: French troops invaded Syria, crushed Arab forces at the <strong>Battle of Maysalun (July 1920)</strong>, and forcibly expelled Feisal from his capital. To manage widespread, violent uprisings against British rule in Baghdad, British Colonial Secretary <strong>Winston Churchill</strong> convened the 1921 Cairo Conference, installing the displaced Feisal as King of newly invented Iraq and his brother Abdullah as Emir of Transjordan, while maintaining tight British military and financial control.<br><br><span class="para-ref">[4.3]</span> The historical verdict on Britain’s wartime diplomacy and imperial cartography is devastating. Modern historians such as <strong>Eugene Rogan</strong> (<em>The Fall of the Ottomans</em>) and <strong>Avi Shlaim</strong> (<em>The Iron Wall</em>) demonstrate that Britain made three mutually irreconcilable pledges between 1915 and 1917: promising full independence to the Arabs (McMahon), promising colonial partition to France (Sykes-Picot), and promising a national home to the Jewish people (Balfour). By drawing arbitrary frontiers across the desert to satisfy imperial greed and oil interests, British and French cartographers created artificial nation-states that combined hostile ethnic, religious, and sectarian communities (such as Sunnis, Shias, and Kurds in Iraq) while dividing others. In Palestine, Britain’s incorporation of the Balfour Declaration into its League of Nations Mandate terms triggered immediate, violent clashes between Jewish immigrants and indigenous Palestinian Arabs throughout the 1920s and 1930s. The "lines in the sand" drawn during the Great War dismantled a four-hundred-year-old Ottoman order and replaced it with a volatile, fractured geopolitical landscape that remains the epicentre of global conflict in the twenty-first century.',
